INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - AUCTION DAY

Oilcloth on the table. Ladder-back chairs. A wall clock that ran slow for fifty years. Outside, through the screen door, trucks and auctioneer chatter in the yard.

Five siblings crowd the room. IVO LANDRY (50s, eldest, Sunday jacket, no tie) stands at the counter packing a box of cutlery. PETRA SZABO (40s, nurse's clogs, keys in a fist) leans on the sink, arms crossed. DOLORES NKRUMAH (late 30s, silk blouse, phone face-down on the table) sits in their mother's chair. ANSELM WHITFIELD (30s, work boots, farm cap) rolls a pencil between his fingers by the stove. KIRSTEN BJORNSEN (20s, youngest, bare feet, a cardboard box in her arms) hovers in the doorway to the hall.

IVO
The clock goes to me.

PETRA
It goes to whoever's here.

ANSELM
I'm here.

DOLORES
I'm here too.

IVO
You're on your phone.

DOLORES
I'm on my phone in the chair.

Anselm sets the pencil on the table. Dolores picks it up and sets it back where it was. Petra jingles her keys. Ivo wraps a fork in newspaper, then another, working quickly, like a man counting something.

KIRSTEN
(from the doorway)
I took the quilt.

PETRA
Which quilt?

KIRSTEN
The blue one.

IVO
That's Ma's quilt.

KIRSTEN
She told me.

ANSELM
When?

KIRSTEN
In March.

DOLORES
Of course she told you.

PETRA
That's not fair.

DOLORES
I didn't say it was fair.

Kirsten shifts the box. Under the lid, a corner of blue appears. Ivo sees it. Looks away. A truck engine roars past the screen door and fades. Ivo puts down the fork and lowers himself, slowly, onto the last free chair.

IVO
The clock goes to whoever winds it.

PETRA
Nobody winds it.

ANSELM
I wind it.

Silence. Dolores lifts her head, surprised. Anselm picks the pencil up again, rolls it between finger and thumb. Petra's hand loosens on the keys, and Kirsten sets the box on the table and, with the care of someone handling an egg, unfolds the quilt. She lays it over the oilcloth. The five of them look at it, a field of faded blue squares.

DOLORES
That's the barn.

ANSELM
That's the pump house.

PETRA
That's the pump house, Ivo.

IVO
(softly)
That's the barn.

Nobody argues. Outside the auctioneer cries a number, and another, and the clock on the wall, for no reason, ticks once and falls silent.
